	        	<div id="350841733219030189-blog" class="blog-body" style="float: left;"> 
		<div id="wsite-content">	<div id="blog-post-335780627638721304" class="blog-post">
	
	
		<div class="blog-header">
			<h2 class="blog-title">
					<a class="blog-title-link blog-link" href="//www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/testimony-in-support-of-house-bill-5004">Testimony in Support of House Bill 5004</a>

			</h2>
			<p class="blog-date">
					<span class="date-text">
		3/3/2025
	</span>

			</p>
			<p class="blog-comments">
					<a href="//www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/testimony-in-support-of-house-bill-5004#comments" class="blog-link">
		1 Comment
	</a>

			</p>
		</div>
	
		<div class="blog-separator">&nbsp;</div>
	
		<div class="blog-content">
				<div><div class="wsite-image wsite-image-border-none " style="padding-top:10px;padding-bottom:10px;margin-left:0;margin-right:0;text-align:center"> <a> <img src="/uploads/2/3/5/3/23532730/screenshot-20250303-162805-youtube_orig.jpg" alt="Picture" style="width:auto;max-width:100%" /> </a> <div style="display:block;font-size:90%"></div> </div></div>  <div class="paragraph"><span><span style="font-weight:700">H.B. 5004 - An Act Concerning the Protection of the Environment and the Development&nbsp;</span></span><br /><span><span style="font-weight:700">of Renewable Energy Sources and Associated Job Sectors&nbsp;<br /></span></span><span style="font-weight:700">Testimony of Alan Cavagnaro</span><span><span style="font-weight:700"><br /></span></span><span style="font-weight:700">March 3, 2025&nbsp;</span><span><span style="font-weight:700"></span></span><br /><br /><span><span>Dear Co-Chairs Lopes and Parker, Ranking Members Harding and Callahan, and members of</span></span><br /><span><span>the Environment Committee,&nbsp;<br /><br />&#8203;</span></span><span>My name is Alan Cavagnaro. I am a student at the University of Connecticut, a Planning and Zoning Commissioner from South Windsor, and the Deputy Director of DesegregateCT. Tonight, I am testifying on my own behalf, representing myself. I am in full support of <a href="https://www.cga.ct.gov/2025/TOB/H/PDF/2025HB-05004-R01-HB.PDF" target="_blank">House Bill 5004</a>.</span><br /><br /><span><span>Climate change is a real and urgent threat that affects everyone, including future generations. House Bill 5004 addresses this by incentivizing clean energy projects and setting new greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions standards, intending to achieve net-zero emissions by 2050. This bill also takes steps to modernize our energy infrastructure in ways that are both cost-effective and environmentally responsible.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>The future of Connecticut&rsquo;s energy system must prioritize efficiency, affordability, and sustainability. H.B. 5004 includes provisions that will lower consumer costs by advancing energy-efficient appliances, expanding clean heating solutions like heat pumps, and investing in state buildings that generate their own electricity through solar. Additionally, supporting solar canopies over large parking lots and the development of thermal energy networks will make clean energy more accessible while reducing strain on the grid.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>I strongly support the provisions in H.B. 5004 that advance Connecticut&rsquo;s decarbonization efforts, particularly through renewable energy expansion and electrification. The transition away from natural gas toward clean energy sources such as solar and wind is essential not only for meeting our updated GHG reduction targets but also for ensuring energy security and economic stability.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>Connecticut already faces some of the highest electricity rates in the country, and continued reliance on natural gas, especially given supply volatility and aging infrastructure, only exacerbates this burden on ratepayers. Investing in solar energy and battery storage will help stabilize long-term energy costs while reducing harmful emissions and creating good-paying jobs in the clean energy sector. As municipalities play a key role in permitting and land use decisions for renewable energy projects, we must align local zoning policies with the state&rsquo;s broader decarbonization goals to accelerate the deployment of clean energy infrastructure.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>On a more personal note, I recently returned from Baku, Azerbaijan, where I attended the United Nations Climate Change Conference (COP29) alongside other students as part of UConn&rsquo;s fellowship program. There, I saw firsthand why climate action is imperative for change. During this time, I met local residents of Baku who shared their concerns about the incoming Trump administration and the potential policies and decisions that could undermine global trust in our collective efforts for a sound and sustainable future.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>Leaders from around the world are taking steps to reduce emissions, invest in clean energy, and prepare for the realities of a warming planet. Connecticut must do the same. We as a country cannot afford to lag behind when the consequences of inaction, such as rising temperatures, extreme weather, and economic instability, are already impacting our communities.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>Beyond environmental benefits, these measures will create jobs in emerging energy sectors and help our communities adapt to the realities of climate change. Connecticut has a unique opportunity to lead on these issues, setting an example for others. Passing House Bill 5004 is a critical step in building a better tomorrow.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>Thank you for considering my testimony.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>Sincerely,</span></span><br /><span><span>Alan Cavagnaro</span></span><br /><br /><em>This written testimony was submitted to the Connecticut General Assembly's Joint Environment Committee for the public hearing on March 3, 2025. Testimony from that hearing can be found&nbsp;<a href="https://www.cga.ct.gov/aspx/CGADisplayTestimonies/CGADisplayTestimony.aspx?bill=HB-05004&amp;doc_year=2025" target="_blank">here</a>.</em></div>

